# ISO

ISO is the camera sensor's sensitivity to light, measured in numbers: 100, 200, 400, 800, 1600, 3200, 6400, 12800, 25600.

## How It Works

- **Lower ISO (100)**: LESS sensitive, CLEAN images, needs more light
- **Higher ISO (6400)**: MORE sensitive, NOISY images, needs less light

## Common Uses

| ISO | Use Case | Effect |
|-----|----------|--------|
| 100 – 200 | Bright daylight, tripod | Cleanest images |
| 400 – 800 | Overcast, indoor | Good balance |
| 1600 – 3200 | Low light, indoor | Acceptable noise |
| 6400 – 12800 | Very low light | Visible noise |
| 25600+ | Extreme low light | Heavy noise |

ISO is the third pillar of the [[exposure-triangle]], alongside [[aperture]] and [[shutter-speed]]. Higher ISO values can introduce noise that may require correction in [[post-processing]]. See [[exposure-triangle]] for how ISO interacts with aperture and shutter speed.